<p>President Trump hosted a “Shield of the Americas” summit at his Miami-area Doral golf club on March 7, bringing together leaders from a dozen Latin American and Caribbean countries. Multiple outlets described the gathering as the launch of a new regional security coalition aimed at confronting drug cartels and transnational gangs. The White House pitched it as a hemispheric partnership against organized crime, and Trump argued the region’s “tremendous potential” depends on defeating cartels and criminal gangs. At the summit, Trump urged participating governments to use “hard power” and floated direct US military support. Several reports said he even raised the possibility of US missile strikes on cartel targets. Trump also used the meeting to make broader regional comments, including remarks about Cuba’s leadership and statements that US-Venezuela diplomatic ties were being restored alongside recognition of an interim Venezuelan government, as reported by regional and US-focused outlets.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/r5Ontbf3hSX41Dw9hF5-rlIXXh6AarrLP0CM9e68gTSVQoCT8TRRvWIF3StV-27wIXgQ5jLfrZlf3aoMaaIRl_I5pEYYcQ7SnurH76YXiyVG' alt='US President Donald Trump poses with Latin American and Caribbean leaders at the Shield of the Americas Summit at Trump National Doral in Miami, Florida, March 7, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>ISIS comparison: The Guardian said Trump argued that “just as” a coalition was formed to eradicate ISIS, a new coalition is needed to “eradicate the cartels”.</li><li>Security framing: PBS said Trump called cartels and transnational gangs an “unacceptable threat” to the hemisphere’s national security and pressed leaders to take military action as part of the effort.</li><li>Cuba remarks: Mercopress and SCMP said Trump claimed Cuba’s communist-led government was nearing collapse and suggested Havana wanted to negotiate with Washington.</li><li>Envoy role: Bloomberg said special envoy Kristi Noem is leading the multinational military-cooperation initiative.</li><li>Iran backdrop: US outlets noted the Miami meeting came as the administration was also focused on a war with Iran, adding to the sense of a wide-ranging foreign-policy agenda.</li></ul><blockquote>You want us to use a missile? We’ll use missiles. They’re extremely accurate - Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>White House: CBS News said officials billed the Miami gathering as a formal “Shield of the Americas” summit even as the administration was also focused on the war with Iran, signaling an effort to keep Western Hemisphere priorities in view at the same time. (<a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/trump-shield-of-the-americas-summit/'>CBS News</a>)</li><li>Financial Times: The outlet highlighted limits to the coalition’s reach, reporting that Brazil, Colombia and Mexico were excluded — a sign that key regional players remain outside the initiative. (<a href='https://www.ft.com/content/b974477f-61a4-492e-b8b7-9709f4086b4c'>Financial Times</a>)</li><li>Irene Mia (IISS): The Latin America expert said several right-leaning heads of state share concerns about rising cartel power, including in countries such as Ecuador and Chile that until recently were seen as relatively safe. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1979526/they-are-extremely-accurate-trump-offers-latin-american-leaders-us-missile-strikes-to-hit-drug-cartels'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>The Guardian: The outlet said Trump explicitly linked the summit to countering China’s economic and political influence in the region, framing security cooperation as part of a broader strategic contest. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/mar/07/trump-shield-of-americas-summit'>The Guardian</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/shield-of-the-americas-trump-launches-regional-coalition/a-76262744'>&#x27;Shield of the Americas&#x27;: Trump launches regional coalition</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/